A shipbroker is the intermediary who brings together those with a cargo and those with a ship, and runs the freight and contract negotiation between them. A chartering broker finds the right ship or cargo, negotiates the freight and charter party terms for the client, and after the fixture stays with the voyage until laytime, demurrage and claims are closed. The commission is usually paid by the shipowner, as a percentage of the freight or hire.

This guide explains a chartering broker's daily work, how brokers earn, and what to look for when working with one, with examples from the tanker and LPG markets.

## What kinds of shipbrokers are there?

- **Chartering brokers** match cargo with ships: voyage charters, time charters and contracts of affreightment (COA). That is the subject of this guide.
- **Sale and purchase brokers** handle second-hand, newbuilding and demolition sales of ships.
- **Charterer's broker and owner's broker:** on a fixture the broker represents the cargo owner or the shipowner; sometimes a single broker sits between the two, sometimes each side has its own.

The markets are separate too: tanker, gas, dry bulk and container markets work with different ships, different contract forms and different players. A broker's depth shows in the market they specialise in.

## Is a shipbroker the same as a ship agent?

No, and it is one of the most common confusions. The **broker** makes the commercial deal: negotiating which ship carries which cargo, on which dates, at what freight and on what terms. The **agent** serves the ship in port once the deal is done: harbour master and customs formalities, pilotage and towage, coordination with the terminal, supplies and crew matters. A voyage needs both; one runs the contract, the other runs the port operation.

## What does a chartering broker do day to day?

- **Watches the market.** Tracks which ships open where and when (position lists), which cargoes are in the market and at what levels the latest fixtures were done.
- **Takes the inquiry and screens ships.** Finds suitable ships for the grade, quantity, ports and laycan, and removes those that fail terminal, vetting or compliance requirements.
- **Compares offers.** Converts freight ideas for ships in different positions into a time charter equivalent (TCE), so a ship that looks cheap but will wait longer does not win by mistake.
- **Negotiates.** Settles the main terms through firm offers and counter-offers; the fixture becomes firm when the subjects, such as ship approval, are lifted.
- **Prepares the recap and the charter party.** Writes everything agreed into a recap and drafts the charter party on the agreed form with its riders.
- **Follows the voyage.** Tracks the post-fixture work: the notice of readiness (NOR), the laytime calculation, the demurrage claim and collecting documents on time.

The steps in detail are in our [step-by-step guide to chartering a tanker](/guides/how-to-charter-a-tanker).

## How does a fixture move through a broker?

Take a simple example. An LPG importer in the Marmara region is looking for a ship for a propane parcel from the Black Sea. In the morning they send their broker the cargo, the terminal, the quantity and the laycan. The broker lists suitable ships opening in the area on those dates, removes ships the terminal will not accept and parties that would fail compliance screening, converts the remaining options to a time charter equivalent including likely waiting at the Bosphorus, and presents them to the client.

The importer chooses a ship; the broker makes a firm offer to the owner, and after a few rounds of counters the freight, laytime, demurrage rate and charter party form are agreed. When the ship approval and terminal acceptance subjects are lifted, the fixture is firm and the broker sends the recap the same day. While the ship loads and transits the Bosphorus the broker follows events; after discharge they collect the statement of facts (SOF), calculate laytime and submit any demurrage claim within the time bar.

At every step the client's job is to make the commercial decisions; the research, negotiation, correspondence and document follow-up are the broker's.

## How does a broker earn, and who pays the commission?

A broker's income is commission (brokerage). It is written into the charter party as a percentage of the freight or hire and is usually paid by the shipowner. Charterers often also receive an address commission. So for a cargo owner looking for a ship, sending an inquiry to a broker is free; the broker's job is to find the client the most suitable ship in the market on the right terms.

## Who charters the ship: the buyer or the seller?

The delivery term in the sale contract (Incoterms) decides. On an FOB purchase the buyer charters the ship and carries the freight risk; on a CFR or CIF sale the seller charters it and builds the freight into the price. Many importers buy CFR for years and never deal with chartering; when they move to FOB, responsibility for the laycan, terminal fit, demurrage and compliance checks suddenly becomes theirs. Talking to a broker before changing the delivery term shows the freight risk and the hidden costs up front.

## Why work with a broker rather than directly with an owner?

- **Market reach.** A single owner offers you its own ships; a broker sees every ship in the market that fits your dates.
- **Comparison.** Offers are converted to the same yardstick (TCE), which makes bunkers, waiting and port costs visible.
- **Contract knowledge.** A broker knows which clauses of forms such as Asbatankvoy, Shellvoy or GASVOY carry risk for you; we explain the differences in our [guide to tanker charter party forms](/guides/tanker-charter-party-forms).
- **Time.** The broker takes on the negotiation, correspondence and document follow-up, so you can focus on the commercial decision.
- **Post-fixture support.** Demurrage and claims are often where a voyage's real money is discussed; a good broker collects the documents during the voyage.

## How do you choose a good shipbroker?

- **Specialism:** are they in the market every day for your cargo and ship type?
- **Response time:** on a live fixture minutes matter. We reply within 60 minutes during business hours.
- **Transparency:** do they say clearly which side they represent and which assumptions their freight idea rests on?
- **Compliance process:** do they run sanctions and price cap checks before offering? Ours is on our [compliance page](/compliance).
- **Regional knowledge:** do they build the Straits transit and its waiting into the estimate for Black Sea cargoes?

## What should you include when sending a broker an inquiry?

{% callout label="Short checklist" linkHref="/contact" linkLabel="Send an inquiry" %}
The grade, quantity and tolerance; the load and discharge ports or terminals; the laycan; spot, time charter or COA; your charter party form if you have one; ship requirements; and the full legal names of the parties.
{% /callout %}

